# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the Vayu engine.
#
# THE RULE: never suppress engine code. The busy-poll event loop, the SPSC
# queues and the relaxed-atomic MetricsCollector are precisely what the weekly
# TSan run is pointed at, and #129 - a writer-vs-writer histogram race that no
# ordinary test could see - is the reason. A race reported in engine/src is a
# bug to fix, not a line to add here.
#
# The one legitimate entry is a *third-party* frame: the vcpkg dependencies are
# built without instrumentation, so TSan cannot observe the synchronization
# inside them and can report a happens-before edge it never saw (OpenSSL's
# locking callbacks and curl's global init are the usual candidates).
#
# Every entry must carry, in a comment directly above it:
#   1. the `WARNING: ThreadSanitizer:` report it silences, trimmed to the frames
#      that matter;
#   2. why the frame cannot be fixed here (upstream, uninstrumented, by design);
#   3. the issue number tracking its removal, if one is expected.
# An entry without those three is removed on sight - the same discipline #897
# put on `// NOLINT` pragmas.
#
# Syntax is one `race:<substring>`, `deadlock:<substring>`,
# `called_from_lib:<lib>` etc. per line; see
# https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions
#
# Two entries, below.

# WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
#   Read of size 8 by thread T11:
#     #0 memcmp
#     #2 match_key crypto/hashtable/hashtable.c:549
#     #3 httplib::SSLServer::process_and_close_socket(int)
#   Previous write by the accepting thread, same hashtable bucket.
#
# OpenSSL's own lock-free property hashtable, reached through httplib's TLS
# accept path. OpenSSL is a vcpkg static dependency built without
# instrumentation, so TSan cannot observe the atomics that order these two
# accesses and reports an edge that is really there. Nothing in engine/ is on
# either side of it: frame #3 is the first non-OpenSSL frame and it is
# header-only third-party code.
#
# Not tracked for removal - it goes away when OpenSSL is built instrumented,
# which is not something this repository does.
# Seen on: linux-tsan, once in a full `ctest --preset linux-tsan` run
# (PerClientIdentityFormat/MutualTlsTest.*, gcc 13 / libtsan 2 / OpenSSL 3.6.3).
# It needs the concurrent accepts a loaded suite produces - six runs of that
# test alone did not reproduce it - so this entry is justified by the captured
# trace above rather than by a repeatable before/after.
race:crypto/hashtable/hashtable.c

# WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
#   Read of size 1 by thread T8 (mutexes: write M0):
#     #0 std::ctype<char>::narrow(char, char) const
#          /usr/include/c++/13/bits/locale_facets.h:941
#     #1 std::__detail::_Scanner<char>::_M_scan_normal()
#          /usr/include/c++/13/bits/regex_scanner.tcc:100
#     #7 httplib::detail::parse_status_line(...)
#     #8 httplib::ClientImpl::read_response_line(...) httplib.h:13856
#   SUMMARY: data race in std::ctype<char>::narrow(char, char) const
#
# libstdc++'s narrowing cache, not anything this repository wrote. `narrow`
# memoises char conversions into a mutable table on the facet
# (`_M_narrow[]` plus its `_M_narrow_ok` flag) and fills it without
# synchronization; two threads that both construct a std::regex race on that
# fill. The facet is the process-wide classic locale, so every thread shares
# it. httplib builds a std::regex per response in `parse_status_line`, which
# is why concurrent HTTP clients reach it and nothing else does.
#
# Cannot be fixed here on either side: the racing write is in libstdc++'s
# headers, and the caller is header-only third-party code. No engine frame
# appears anywhere in the stack - frame #7 is the first non-libstdc++ frame
# and it is httplib's.
#
# Tracked in #967. It is a real report rather than an artifact of missing
# instrumentation, so unlike the OpenSSL entry above it is worth an upstream
# fix; the practical remedies are upstream (libstdc++ synchronising the cache,
# or httplib hoisting its regex to a function-local static).
#
# Seen on: linux-tsan, TransportPolicyPaths.LoadRunTraversesManualProxy, gcc 13
# / libtsan 2. Only became visible once #957 was fixed - before that every test
# that opens a socket segfaulted inside the sanitizer runtime and never got far
# enough to report anything.
race:std::ctype<char>::narrow
